sections.s2_technical:
The core idea is that an AWS account is the strongest isolation boundary AWS provides. Resources in one account are, by default, completely separate from another account, so separating environments and workloads into their own accounts contains failures and breaches.

The common structure is separate accounts per environment: a development account where engineers experiment freely, a staging account that mirrors production for testing, and a production account that is tightly locked down. Many organizations go further with an account per team or per workload, plus shared accounts for security tooling, logging, and networking.

AWS Organizations is the governance layer that makes many accounts manageable. It gives consolidated billing, so all accounts roll up into one invoice and volume discounts apply across the whole organization. It provides Service Control Policies, which are organization-wide guardrails that set the maximum permissions any account can have — for example a policy that denies the ability to disable CloudTrail or to create resources outside approved Regions, which even an account's own administrators cannot override. Organizations also lets you group accounts into Organizational Units and apply policies per group.

```java
// A Service Control Policy idea: deny turning off audit logging anywhere.
String scp = """
    {
      "Version": "2012-10-17",
      "Statement": [{
        "Effect": "Deny",
        "Action": ["cloudtrail:StopLogging", "cloudtrail:DeleteTrail"],
        "Resource": "*"
      }]
    }
    """;
```

AWS IAM Identity Center, formerly AWS SSO, solves access across many accounts. Instead of separate IAM users in every account, users sign in once and assume roles into the accounts and permission sets they are entitled to, ideally federated from the corporate identity provider. This centralizes who can access what, makes onboarding and offboarding one action, and avoids long-lived credentials scattered across accounts.

Now why one big account is a bad idea, concretely. There is no blast radius isolation: a runaway script or a compromised credential in development can delete or read production resources because they share the account. Security boundaries are weak, because IAM policies within one account are harder to get perfectly right than the hard wall between accounts. Service quotas and limits are shared, so one team's load test can exhaust capacity for everyone. Cost attribution is muddy, because separating spend by team or environment relies entirely on tagging discipline rather than a clean per-account bill. And a single misconfiguration or breach exposes everything at once.

A senior nuance: the multi-account model is the AWS-recommended landing zone pattern, and tools like AWS Control Tower automate setting it up with a management account, a dedicated log-archive account, and a security/audit account, plus baseline guardrails. The management account itself should hold almost no workloads and be tightly guarded, because it controls the whole organization. The trade-off of many accounts is more structure to manage, which Organizations, SSO, and infrastructure-as-code handle, and that structure is well worth the isolation for anything handling real money.

